<p>In 1848, English poet Arthur Hugh Clough named a new work <em>The Bothie of Toper-na-Fuosich</em>, apparently thinking this was an innocent Gaelic place name. The <em>Literary Gazette</em> informed him that it means &#8220;the hut of the bearded well&#8221; &#8212; it&#8217;s a Highland toast to the female genitalia.</p>
<p>&#8220;Red-faced and embarrassed, Clough was nevertheless ribald enough to giggle delightedly over the appallingly ludicrous way in which he had introduced such unseemliness into &#8216;all the drawing rooms and boudoirs (of course) of all the world,'&#8221; <a href="https://archive.org/details/arthurhughclough0000bisw/page/264/mode/2up">notes author Robindra Kumar Biswas</a>.</p>
<p>On republishing the poem, Clough changed the name to the appropriately meaningless Tober-na-Vuolich.</p>
